verdict after sseeing the answer: correct! There was a cleaner win but my line was also winning. I think the overall takeaway from this is that in an actual classical game, spend just as much time on the follow up moves to find the cleanest conversion/way forward, rather than relying on a variation you calculated at the beginning, even if it's a decent variation. Start each new position from scratch and without past bias. } 1. Bxg7  (1. Rxg7+?? {I correctly saw there was nothing here}  Rxg7 2. Rxg7+ Kf8) Kh7  (1... Rxg7  2. Rxg7+ Kf8 3. Rg8+ Kf7 4. R2g7+ Kf6  (4... Ke6  5. Ng3 b5 6. Re8+ Kd6 7. Nxe4+ Kc6 8. Rg6+ Kc7 9. Ng3 h5 10. a4 b4) 5. Ng3 {I also correctly evaluated this variation as winning for me, black has no way to entre and faces constant threats from my 3 pieces which swarm it. } Bc8 6. Rxc8 Kxg7 7. Nf5+)  (1... Bxg2  2. Bf6+ Kh7 3. Bxh4 Kxg6 4. Kxg2 Rd7 {this line I saw flawlessly up until rd7 where my vision got a bit hazy. I didn't know how to stop the rook infiltration} 5. Ng3  (5. Kf2?! {this I saw in my calculations but was very worried of the rook activity. As a positional player that tries to prevent counterplay, I was honed in on killing his counterplay, but wasn't calculating my own active ideas once I realized the infiltration was unstoppable}  Rd1 6. Ke2 Ra1  (6... Rc1  7. a4 Rb1 8. Nd2 {I didn't see that white has a defensive configuration to kill all the counterplay after all!} Rh1  (8... Rb2  9. Bd8)) 7. a4 Rb1) Rd2+ 6. Kf1 Rxa2 7. f5+ Kf7 8. Nxe4 Rxh2 9. Nd6+ Kg8 10. Be7 {the answer to this is not to try and save my queenside pawns, white must play actively by winning e4 and the marching the pawns down the board, black won't have time to win the pawns and push his own. lesson: If you cannot prevent an infiltration or a plan, often times the best way forward is to ruthlessly follow your own plans and play actively}) 2. Bf6  (2. R2g3! {cleaner was this, rxg6 is threatened and can't be stopped. }  Bxf1 3. Kxf1 Qxh2 4. Rxh6+ Qxh6 5. Bxh6 Kxh6 6. Rg5 {[%cal Gg5e5,Gf1e2]with black's king completely cut off, this endgame should be trivially winning. }) Qxf6 3. Rxf6 Rxf6 4. Rg3 {the line I saw during analysis, which I correctly evaluated as better for white due to the passed c pawn. } *

